          "note": "Sub-heading 'IPv6' of u-ipv4-and-ipv6 as printed (PDF 15-16, printed 10-11)."
        }
      ],
      "body": {
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Example: 2001:0db8:85a3:0000:0000:8a2e:0370:7334"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "IPv6 has 8 groups of 4 hexadecimal digits, separated by colons."
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Each group = 16 bits"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Total = 128 bits"
          },
          {
            "type": "table",
            "caption": "Breakdown of IPv6",
            "header": [
              "Part",
              "Value",
              "What it means"
            ],
            "rows": [
              [
                "Global routing prefix",
                "2001:0db8:85a3",
                "ISP/region, like “Pakistan, Sindh, Karachi”"
              ],
              [
                "Subnet ID",
                "0000",
                "Your local network inside the ISP, like “Gulshan area”"
              ],
              [
                "Interface ID",
                "0000:8a2e:0370:7334",
                "Your specific device, like “Your laptop”"
              ]
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "tone": "note",
            "title": "Do You Know?",
            "text": "• IPv6 addresses comprise: 2¹²⁸ ≈ 3.4 × 10³⁸ total addresses.\n• IPv6 will never run out; it is an astronomically massive number."
          }
        ]
      }
    }
  ]
